{0 Hover}

Show a preview of the link target's content on hover.

{1 Input}

A cursor position (0-based line and character) in a markdown file that
belongs to a vault with a built index.

{1 Link detection}

Same link forms as {!page-"feature-go-to-definition"}: wikilinks,
markdown links, markdown images.  External URLs are excluded.

{1 Response}

If the cursor falls on a recognized link, return a
[textDocument/hover] response:

{t
  | Field          | Value                                            |
  |----------------|--------------------------------------------------|
  | contents       | Markdown string (see below)                      |
  | range          | byte range of the link                           |
}

{2 Contents format}

The hover content is a Markdown string with [MarkupKind.Markdown].

Every hover result begins with a {b path header} of the form
[*Path*:<relative-path>]. For self-referencing fragments the current
file's path is used.

When the target file is empty, the hover shows the path header followed
by [*(empty)*].

{ul
  {- {b Resolved note / file link} ([[[note]]]): path header, then the
     raw markdown content of the target file.}
  {- {b Resolved heading fragment} ([[[note#Heading]]]): path header,
     then the source of the matched heading's section: the heading and what
     follows it in the same container, up to (but not including) the next
     heading of equal or higher level. These are the same blocks an embed of
     the link includes ({!Oystermark.Note.read}).}
  {- {b Resolved block-ID fragment} ([[[note#^blockid]]]): path header,
     then the paragraph that carries the block ID, or the block before a
     block ID written on a line of its own.}
  {- {b Self-referencing fragments} ([[[#Heading]]], [[[#^blockid]]]):
     same as above but sourced from the current file.}
  {- {b Resolved image} ([[![[pic.png]]]], [[![](pic.png)]]): path header,
     then a description or a preview.  See {!section-images}.}
  {- {b Resolved non-text file} (a PDF, say): path header, then a
     description.  See {!section-binary}.}
  {- {b Unresolved link}: no hover result (return [None]).}
}

{2:images Images}

A link whose target has an image extension ([.png], [.jpg], [.jpeg],
[.gif], [.svg], [.webp]) is never shown as text: its bytes are not text,
and a hover full of them is both unreadable and invalid UTF-8 in the
response.

The default is a {b description}: the path header, then a line naming
the format, the pixel dimensions when they can be read from the file's
header, and the file size.

{[
*Path*:assets/diagram.png

PNG · 1024×768 · 84 KB
]}

Dimensions come from the container header — [IHDR] for PNG, the logical
screen descriptor for GIF, the first [SOFn] frame header for JPEG — read
from the leading bytes rather than by decoding the image.  A format whose
dimensions are not read (SVG, WebP), or a file too damaged to parse, omits
that field rather than guessing:

{[
*Path*:assets/logo.svg

SVG · 12 KB
]}

{3:preview Preview}

With {!Lsp_lib.Config.t.hover_image_preview} set, the description line is
followed by the image itself, embedded as a [data:] URI so that the hover
needs nothing from the filesystem at render time:

{[
*Path*:assets/diagram.png

PNG · 1024×768 · 84 KB

![assets/diagram.png](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgo…)
]}

It is {b off by default}, for two reasons.

Rendering is the client's, not ours: [contents] is a Markdown string, and
whether an image in it becomes a picture is entirely up to what the client
renders that string with.  VS Code does render [data:] image URIs in
hovers; an editor that lays the markdown out as text — Neovim's built-in
hover handler, and most terminal clients — shows nothing at all, or the
literal markup.  The setting is therefore a statement about the {e client},
which is why it is a setting rather than a default.

The second reason is size.  Base64 inflates by a third, and the result
travels inline in a synchronous JSON-RPC response that the editor is
blocking a hover on.  So the preview is capped by
{!Lsp_lib.Config.t.hover_image_max_bytes} — measured on the file, before
encoding — and a file over the cap falls back to the description alone,
with a note saying why:

{[
*Path*:assets/photo.jpg

JPEG · 4032×3024 · 6 MB

*(preview omitted: 6 MB exceeds the 256 KB limit)*
]}

The description is never omitted: it is short, it is the part that is
readable everywhere, and it is what makes the fallback legible.
{!Lsp_lib.Config.t.hover_max_chars} does not apply to an image hover —
truncating a data URI would produce a broken image rather than a shorter
one, and the description is already bounded.

{2:binary Non-text files}

A link may resolve to a file that is neither markdown nor an image — a
PDF, an archive, a binary blob.  Reading its bytes into the hover has the
same two problems as an image and none of the compensations, so a file
whose leading bytes contain a NUL is described rather than shown:

{[
*Path*:refs/paper.pdf

Binary file · 2 MB
]}

The NUL test is deliberately crude: it is what [grep] and [git] use, it
costs one scan of the first few kilobytes, and it errs towards showing
text — a file it misjudges as text is truncated as usual rather than
lost.

{2 Truncation}

The budget applies to the {b body} only: the path header is short,
always useful, and survives however small
{!Lsp_lib.Config.t.hover_max_chars} is.

If the body exceeds {!Lsp_lib.Config.t.hover_max_chars} bytes, it is
truncated at that boundary (snapped to the previous newline to avoid
cutting mid-word) and a notice is appended after a blank line, reporting
how much of the body the reader is seeing:

{[
*(truncated: showing 42 of 80 lines, 52%)*
]}

Lines are the unit, because that is what a reader perceives and the cut
lands on a line boundary, so the count is exact. The percentage is
derived from the very same counts, so the two figures can never
disagree.

When one or zero lines are hidden the line counts carry no information
(the remainder is a single long line, e.g. a wide table row: ["1 of 2
lines, 50%"] would hide kilobytes), the notice switches wholesale to
bytes rather than mixing units:

{[
*(truncated: showing 240 B of 12 KB, 2%)*
]}

The percentage is rounded down and clamped to \[1, 99\]: truncation did
happen, so the reader is never told [0%] or [100%].

{1 Edge cases}

{ul
  {- {b Cursor outside any link}: no hover result.}
  {- {b Target file unreadable}: no hover result.}
  {- {b Heading / block not found in target file}: fall back to the full
     file content (subject to truncation), consistent with the
     go-to-definition fallback.}
  {- {b Image with a fragment} ([[![[pic.png#frag]]]]): the fragment is
     ignored — there is nothing in an image for it to name — and the
     image is described as usual.}
  {- {b Empty image file}: described like any other, at [0 B], with no
     preview and no dimensions.  {b Not} [*(empty)*], which is about a
     note with nothing written in it.}
}
